<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epane, Epona Augusta, Epona Regina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epona was the goddess of horses and ponies in Gallo-Roman religion, and also a fertility and mother goddess. Her name, from Gaulish, means 'Great Mare'. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a> she watched over horses, donkeys, mules and their riders, and she was often shown riding sidesaddle or seated between horses or foals, holding a cornucopia, fruit or grain. Cavalry units recruited from Gaul, Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> spread her cult across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, where she was widely worshipped as patroness of cavalry between the 1st and 3rd centuries CE. She was the only Celtic divinity ultimately worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself, invoked for the emperor as Epona Augusta or Epona Regina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epona.jpg" alt="Epona" width="330" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosemania · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Epona, 'Great Mare', comes from Gaulish. It derives from the Proto-Celtic word *ekʷos, 'horse', with an augmentative suffix often found in names of deities, such as Sirona and Matrona. Some scholars suggest instead that the ending refers to sacred water. Horses mattered greatly to Gallic life: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> sent his horses away rather than let them be captured or killed. Iconography suggests that before Roman contact Epona was a peaceful divinity of fertility and protection rather than of war. Some scholars compare her with Rhiannon of the Welsh Mabinogion, but Ronald Hutton is sceptical, since Rhiannon is a much later literary creation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In Gaul Epona was worshipped as a mother goddess who watched over horses, donkeys, mules and their riders, and she was probably also a protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse-breeding</a> and of grooms, muleteers, charioteers and travellers. Altars to her were commonly placed in stables. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-golden-ass">The Golden Ass</a>, describes a shrine to her in a niche of a stable post, wreathed with fresh roses, and Juvenal links her to stables.</p><p>Some objects dedicated to her suggest a funerary role, and Fernand Benoît proposed that her images expressed a theme of victory over death. A story attributed to Pseudo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> tells how a man named Fulvius Stellus, who hated women, had a daughter by a mare, who was called Epona.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> was made Roman, Epona became a protector of cavalry. Military units recruited from Gaul, Lower Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> spread her cult across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and she was the only Celtic divinity ultimately worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself. A rustic calendar from Guidizzolo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> gives her feast day as 18 December, though this may have been only local. She was invoked for the emperor as Epona Augusta or Epona Regina.</p><p>Most evidence comes from eastern and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, in the form of votive objects, small statues and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions, many made by cavalrymen in fulfilment of a vow; objects found by springs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> suggest she was also seen as a healer. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> she was known as Epane, and her cult probably spread along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-mining towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>. Folklorists have linked the May Day hobby-horses of Padstow and Minehead and the Welsh Mari Lwyd to her, but there is no firm evidence for this.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Epona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cernunnos, the horned god, and <b>Epona</b>, goddess of horses, were widely known, and triplicity was common, as in the Three Mothers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions record hundreds of divine names, many of them local: Lugus, remembered in the name of Lugdunum, modern Lyon; <b>Epona</b>, the horse goddess, popular in the Roman army; Cernunnos, named on a carved pillar from Paris; and Sulis, goddess of the hot springs at Bath.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped many gods, among them the thunder god Taranis, Lugus, a god of skills and crafts, and the horse goddess <b>Epona</b>, held ceremonies in sacred groves and offered treasured things in rivers, wetlands and shafts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
